## API Reference

| Name | Description |
|---|---|
| `configureSound({ enabled, volume })` | Toggle and master volume (0–1, clamped). Returns the resulting config |
| `getSoundConfig()` | A copy of the current config |
| `playSound(type \| preset)` | Plays. Returns whether it actually did |
| `sound.click()` / `.key()` / `.toggle()` / `.success()` / `.error()` / `.open()` / `.close()` | Shorthands |
| `isSoundSupported()` | Whether the browser has Web Audio |
| `disposeSound()` | Closes the graph; the next play rebuilds it |
| `SOUND_PRESETS` | The preset table — read the parameters, or start a custom cue from one |

## It Is Off by Default

```ts
import { configureSound, sound } from '@talex-touch/tuffex/utils'

configureSound({ enabled: true, volume: 0.6 })
sound.click()
```

**Nothing plays until you turn it on, and no `AudioContext` is even created.** A component library that starts making noise on import is one the host has to go and disable; opting in is a single call, opting out after a surprise is a bug report.

The graph is built **lazily** for the same reason: browsers park an `AudioContext` created outside a user gesture in `suspended` state, so a page that imports this and never plays anything would be leaking one.

## Why Synthesis Rather Than Audio Files

A handful of oscillators and an envelope cost nothing to ship, stay crisp at any sample rate, and cannot 404. The trade is timbre — samples are richer — but UI feedback should be plain anyway.

## The Presets

Seven, deliberately. A UI that plays seven different sounds is noise.

| Preset | Use | Measured peak | Measured length |
|---|---|---|---|
| `click` | Button press | 0.103 | 40ms |
| `key` | Per character in a field | **0.056** | 27ms |
| `toggle` | Switch or checkbox turning on | 0.089 | 65ms |
| `success` | Completed action (rising pair) | 0.090 | 188ms |
| `error` | Rejected action (falling pair) | 0.100 | 216ms |
| `open` | Panel or dialog opening | 0.078 | 91ms |
| `close` | Panel or dialog closing | 0.077 | 91ms |

Those figures come from rendering each cue through an `OfflineAudioContext` and measuring sample by sample, not from the nominal values. Three design intentions are visible in the data:

- **`key` has the lowest peak.** It fires per character; at `click`'s level it becomes a typewriter.
- **`open` and `close` mirror each other exactly** in peak and length (0.078 / 0.077, both 91ms), differing only in glide direction — direction carries the meaning, loudness does not.
- **Nothing clips**; every peak sits between 0.06 and 0.11.

Two kinds of cue get two budgets, enforced by tests: **immediate feedback** (`click` / `key` / `toggle`) is capped at 80ms so it lands while the finger is still down; **status cues** get 250ms, enough for a two-note figure but not enough to arrive after the user has moved on.

## Custom Cues

`playSound` also takes a patch built on the spot:

```ts
import { playSound } from '@talex-touch/tuffex/utils'

playSound({
  layers: [
    { wave: 'sine', freq: [400, 700], gain: 0.09, decay: 0.12 },
    { wave: 'sine', freq: 900, gain: 0.06, decay: 0.1, delay: 0.08 },
  ],
})
```

Two values in `freq` make a glide; `delay` offsets the second voice so the pair reads as a figure rather than a chord.

## Contract

- **It never throws.** Disabled, unsupported, or before the browser has seen a gesture, `playSound` returns `false` quietly. A feedback channel must never be the reason an interaction fails.
- **Autoplay policy is handled.** A parked context is resumed on the next play, and a rejected resume is swallowed.
- **Nodes are reclaimed.** Each voice disconnects its own envelope when it ends — without that a typing burst leaves hundreds of dead nodes on the master gain.
- **The noise buffer is shared**, not regenerated per keystroke.
- A volume change applies to an existing graph immediately; no rebuild.

## Relationship to Vibration

`useVibrate` is the other feedback channel at this layer, and its shape is deliberately mirrored here (preset table, main function, shorthand object). They compose, but **do not fire sound and vibration for the same action** — doubled feedback reads as having triggered twice.

